00:30:52.900 --> 00:30:54.180
She did our show.

604
00:30:54.480 --> 00:30:56.540
She's done a fair amount of work in animation.

605
00:30:56.540 --> 00:31:01.580
In fact, she's the voice of Tip in the Netflix show Home Adventures with Tip and O.

606
00:31:02.500 --> 00:31:03.540
She's phenomenal.

607
00:31:03.540 --> 00:31:04.540
What a voice.

608
00:31:04.920 --> 00:31:05.280
I know.

609
00:31:05.760 --> 00:31:09.500
Well, I was going to point out one other person in this scene, and that is Clay Aiken.

610
00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:10.260
Mm-hmm.

611
00:31:10.380 --> 00:31:12.720
I was really rooting for him in American Idol.

612
00:31:12.940 --> 00:31:16.520
I will never forget his performance of Bridge Over Troubled Waters.

613
00:31:16.740 --> 00:31:17.700
Oh, right.

614
00:31:18.000 --> 00:31:18.840
So good.

615
00:31:19.000 --> 00:31:19.160
Yeah.

616
00:31:19.300 --> 00:31:20.500
And I brought a clip of that.

617
00:31:20.740 --> 00:31:22.180
I'm loving today.

618
00:31:53.000 --> 00:31:55.200
Oh, that still packs a punch.

619
00:31:55.420 --> 00:31:56.840
I mean, I get chills.

620
00:31:57.180 --> 00:31:58.180
It's so beautiful.

621
00:31:59.100 --> 00:31:59.660
Wow.

622
00:32:00.460 --> 00:32:01.020
Wow.

623
00:32:01.560 --> 00:32:02.680
Well, let's see.

624
00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:06.000
I've got a little location breakdown for you.

625
00:32:06.520 --> 00:32:08.480
We shot this on location.

626
00:32:09.000 --> 00:32:10.780
All of us had the day off.

627
00:32:10.920 --> 00:32:11.920
It was two days off.

628
00:32:12.140 --> 00:32:13.620
Ed filmed these scenes.

629
00:32:13.620 --> 00:32:19.780
They were in Burbank, and we did not get to meet any of these folks on the day.

630
00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:20.980
No, we didn't.

631
00:32:21.100 --> 00:32:21.680
I know.

632
00:32:22.220 --> 00:32:29.600
Well, Andy is going to try to sing for them, and it does not go well at all.

633
00:32:29.880 --> 00:32:36.040
Yes, he enters as the old man, but it's very clear he's not really an old man.

634
00:32:36.400 --> 00:32:37.600
Aaron Rodgers calls him out.

635
00:32:37.720 --> 00:32:39.540
But he's going to go ahead and start singing.

636
00:32:40.020 --> 00:32:42.700
He's going to sing, You're Never Fully Dressed Without a Smile.

637
00:32:42.980 --> 00:32:43.340
Mm-hmm.

638
00:32:43.600 --> 00:32:44.460
And you know what?

639
00:32:44.580 --> 00:32:46.300
They aren't feeling it.

640
00:32:46.400 --> 00:32:47.320
They're not feeling it.

641
00:32:47.320 --> 00:32:52.540
So he quickly switches gears, and he starts singing the Cornell Fight Song.

642
00:32:52.940 --> 00:32:53.300
Yep.

643
00:32:53.660 --> 00:32:56.760
I was curious if that is the actual Cornell Fight Song.

644
00:32:56.920 --> 00:32:57.980
I assume it was.

645
00:32:58.180 --> 00:33:03.320
And I found a compilation of Cornell a cappella groups singing it.

646
00:33:03.600 --> 00:33:04.620
Well, okay.

647
00:33:14.750 --> 00:33:23.890
That's precious.

648
00:33:24.130 --> 00:33:27.390
How perfect is it, though, that they're a cappella groups?

649
00:33:27.710 --> 00:33:28.890
Andy would be so proud.

650
00:33:29.250 --> 00:33:31.090
But the judges are not impressed.

651
00:33:31.350 --> 00:33:32.130
They're not.

652
00:33:32.130 --> 00:33:34.670
And then Andy has a full meltdown.

653
00:33:34.990 --> 00:33:35.130
Yeah.

654
00:33:35.290 --> 00:33:37.190
I can see them all trying not to laugh.

655
00:33:37.390 --> 00:33:37.830
Okay.

656
00:33:38.090 --> 00:33:39.850
All of the judges are trying not to laugh.

657
00:33:40.450 --> 00:33:40.930
Yes.

658
00:33:40.930 --> 00:33:43.250
I asked Santi Gold about this.

659
00:33:43.430 --> 00:33:44.770
Here's what she had to say.

660
00:33:45.070 --> 00:33:52.410
I think, for me, probably the funniest part and the hardest part to hold it together was when I said to him that he couldn't just sit there and cry.

661
00:33:52.490 --> 00:33:55.190
And he's like, I can't so just sit here and cry.

662
00:33:55.350 --> 00:33:57.950
And he's like, really, like, yells it.

663
00:33:57.950 --> 00:34:02.170
But just now watching it, I watched it and, like, I forgot the whole thing.

664
00:34:02.230 --> 00:34:03.170
And it's so funny.

665
00:34:03.490 --> 00:34:05.210
I mean, he did it differently every time.

666
00:34:05.330 --> 00:34:08.370
So every time was, like, new opportunities to crack up laughing.

667
00:34:08.370 --> 00:34:08.730
So.

668
00:34:09.150 --> 00:34:10.610
But I think that's part of the fun of it.

669
00:34:10.690 --> 00:34:12.990
To have to hold it together is also the fun of it.

670
00:34:13.090 --> 00:34:16.150
And to come up with, like, other things to do besides laugh.

671
00:34:17.090 --> 00:34:18.210
Well, I really enjoyed it.

672
00:34:18.310 --> 00:34:23.750
I mean, it was kind of, like, everything that I thought that I wanted it to be, it was.

673
00:34:24.230 --> 00:34:28.750
And I remember talking to my friend who was, like, my main friend that we loved The Office together.

674
00:34:29.150 --> 00:34:31.650
And I told him that I was on The Office and he didn't believe me.

675
00:34:31.690 --> 00:34:33.170
And he's like, you're kidding me, you know?

676
00:34:33.310 --> 00:34:36.150
Because it was, like, a dream for that to be the first thing to do.

677
00:34:36.550 --> 00:34:41.670
And also, because it's so many years later now, a lot of people had no idea that I was ever even on there.

678
00:34:41.850 --> 00:34:45.850
And, you know, The Office has had such a continually growing fan base.

679
00:34:45.970 --> 00:34:49.630
It seems like it had a whole new life all over again, which is great.

680
00:34:49.710 --> 00:34:50.330
It's, like, a legacy.

681
00:34:51.110 --> 00:34:55.390
And so people have discovered me on the end, on the end episodes.

682
00:34:55.390 --> 00:34:56.850
And I'm like, oh, my God, are you in The Office?

683
00:34:56.890 --> 00:35:00.550
It's really funny because it's so unexpected and it's such a nice surprise.

684
00:35:00.670 --> 00:35:04.370
So it just keeps surprising people all these years later.

685
00:35:06.820 --> 00:35:12.220
So, Angela, since I knew that this was Santi's first on-camera acting job,

686
00:35:12.420 --> 00:35:16.400
I just kind of asked her in general, like, how was that, like, coming onto the set?

687
00:35:16.600 --> 00:35:19.100
And she told a story that I think you'll enjoy.

688
00:35:19.340 --> 00:35:20.100
Here it is.

689
00:35:21.620 --> 00:35:25.280
Yeah, so the day of, I remember coming there.

690
00:35:25.380 --> 00:35:27.820
They told us to wear our own clothes because we were supposed to be playing ourselves.

691
00:35:28.000 --> 00:35:29.520
So that part was simple.

692
00:35:29.520 --> 00:35:33.120
And we just got, you know, just got out of hair and makeup and then went in.

693
00:35:33.220 --> 00:35:35.080
And it was just me, Clay Aiken, and Aaron Rodgers.

694
00:35:35.080 --> 00:35:38.360
And I knew who Clay Aiken was, but I did not know who Aaron Rodgers was.

695
00:35:38.540 --> 00:35:40.420
And I don't know anything about football.

696
00:35:41.440 --> 00:35:43.200
So I asked him, I said, oh, what do you do?

697
00:35:43.940 --> 00:35:45.720
And he's like, oh, I'm a football player.

698
00:35:46.040 --> 00:35:47.880
And I said, what team do you play for?

699
00:35:48.020 --> 00:35:51.320
And he said, I believe he said the Green Bay Packers, if that's a team.

700
00:35:51.420 --> 00:35:51.980
I'm so bad.

701
00:35:52.000 --> 00:35:53.180
I still don't know about football.

702
00:35:54.120 --> 00:35:56.600
And then I asked him, what city was that?

703
00:35:56.740 --> 00:35:57.580
And he told me.

704
00:35:57.660 --> 00:35:58.900
And for some reason, I thought he was joking.

705
00:35:58.900 --> 00:36:01.040
And I was like, no, for real, what city is it?

706
00:36:01.040 --> 00:36:02.700
And he's like, no, that really is it.

707
00:36:03.480 --> 00:36:04.900
So it's kind of a funny intro.

708
00:36:05.040 --> 00:36:08.500
We ended up getting along really well and having a lot of fun throughout the day.

709
00:36:10.440 --> 00:36:17.580
But yeah, it was, I mean, I remember that they were changing the lines a lot, which made me feel a little bit anxious because I'm not used to that.

710
00:36:17.580 --> 00:36:23.340
And obviously didn't have any experience with that, with, I mean, with any of it at all.

711
00:36:23.480 --> 00:36:32.520
It was also new, but like the improvisational aspect was so actually fun to be in and watch Ed Helms just master.

712
00:36:32.880 --> 00:36:33.700
It was so funny.

713
00:36:34.200 --> 00:36:46.220
And yeah, it was hard not to laugh, but at the same time, it was fun to just be in it with him and just get to experience it and not fall apart.

714
00:36:47.980 --> 00:36:54.140
But we did it obviously several times and every time he did it different and every time it was like hilarious.

715
00:36:55.360 --> 00:37:03.120
And so I always, I don't know, I always just really have a fun time watching people who master their crafts and just being able to just be a witness of it.

716
00:37:03.160 --> 00:37:12.700
So I remember the experience being more like me getting to watch him and really, really enjoying that and just being a part of it at all, you know.

717
00:37:14.140 --> 00:37:21.960
Lady, I just love this runner of people asking Aaron Rodgers, what is it that you do?

718
00:37:22.140 --> 00:37:22.960
Oh, sub all.

719
00:37:23.380 --> 00:37:24.580
That's so neat.

720
00:37:24.880 --> 00:37:26.280
Like, I don't know why.

721
00:37:26.280 --> 00:37:28.080
It's just very funny to me.

722
00:37:28.840 --> 00:37:31.980
Well, in my experience, he was really kind in the moment.

723
00:37:32.980 --> 00:37:38.320
Well, Dave Rodgers said all three of these guys were so great to work with.

724
00:37:38.520 --> 00:37:42.780
He remembered that Clay Aiken was really funny and had a great sense of humor.

725
00:37:42.780 --> 00:37:45.240
And he said he was really great at doing improv.

726
00:37:46.220 --> 00:37:50.440
Dave said that Aaron Rodgers was really down to earth, super friendly.

727
00:37:50.580 --> 00:37:51.880
He came in really prepared.

728
00:37:52.040 --> 00:37:54.380
He knew all his lines and gave great reactions.

729
00:37:54.380 --> 00:37:57.800
He really came off very relaxed and natural.

730
00:37:58.280 --> 00:38:06.400
And he said that our editor, Claire Scanlon, was a huge fan of Santigold and was so bummed she couldn't be there.

731
00:38:06.820 --> 00:38:15.720
So Dave asked if Santigold would mind writing her a note and autographing it and giving it to Claire.

732
00:38:16.060 --> 00:38:18.340
And Claire was over the moon.

733
00:38:18.560 --> 00:38:20.940
That is so sweet of Dave and so thoughtful.

734
00:38:21.240 --> 00:38:21.660
I know.

735
00:38:21.660 --> 00:38:24.260
But also so nice of Santigold to be like, sure, no problem.

736
00:38:24.440 --> 00:38:24.960
I know.

737
00:38:25.220 --> 00:38:29.300
Well, I want to give a big thank you to Santigold for sending in all those audio clips.

738
00:38:29.500 --> 00:38:31.880
You know, she has a podcast as well, Ange.

739
00:38:31.940 --> 00:38:33.560
It's called Noble Champions.

740
00:38:34.160 --> 00:38:36.080
And here's how it is described.

741
00:38:36.800 --> 00:38:43.420
Noble Champions is a modern-day salon created and hosted by the multi-platform visionary artist Santigold.

742
00:38:43.640 --> 00:38:48.620
In each episode, she sits down with some of today's leading artists, authors, activists,

743
00:38:48.620 --> 00:38:53.980
and progressive thinkers who stand up, stick up, and speak up for important causes.

744
00:38:54.320 --> 00:38:58.460
The first season is out now, and she's going to have new episodes premiering this fall.

745
00:38:58.620 --> 00:38:59.220
So check it out.

746
00:38:59.480 --> 00:38:59.680
Great.

747
00:38:59.780 --> 00:39:00.940
We'll put that in our stories.

748
00:39:01.240 --> 00:39:03.440
I would love to give her a shout-out as well.

749
00:39:03.440 --> 00:39:09.140
Well, I love NPR's Tiny Desk Concert series, and Santigold's is so great.

750
00:39:09.380 --> 00:39:14.660
So if you're a fan of that series and haven't seen hers, it's a great watch, and I'll put that in stories as well.

751
00:39:14.840 --> 00:39:15.180
Great.

752
00:39:15.540 --> 00:39:16.640
Well, why don't we go to break?

753
00:39:16.640 --> 00:39:20.360
Because when we come back, Philip and Kevin are finally going to make amends.

754
00:39:20.720 --> 00:39:28.320
Well, I want to go to break because, Angela, you brought in, like, two coolers worth of food today.

755
00:39:28.320 --> 00:39:31.220
And I've been waiting patiently to get to eat it.

756
00:39:31.340 --> 00:39:32.780
Will you tell everybody what it is?

757
00:39:33.420 --> 00:39:35.760
So Josh and I sold a cookbook.

758
00:39:35.920 --> 00:39:36.920
I've shared that with you guys.

759
00:39:37.020 --> 00:39:37.140
Yeah.

760
00:39:37.180 --> 00:39:39.380
And this week is the cookbook photography.

761
00:39:39.900 --> 00:39:46.580
And what that means is we're making, like, 11 recipes a day and then professionally food styling and taking the photos.

762
00:39:46.580 --> 00:39:48.880
It's turning out so fantastic.

763
00:39:49.180 --> 00:39:53.600
But it also means every day Josh is bringing home, like, 11 recipes.

764
00:39:54.380 --> 00:39:55.780
My fridge was so full.

765
00:39:55.780 --> 00:39:58.240
So today I said, you guys, I'm bringing in food for everyone.

766
00:39:58.440 --> 00:40:00.520
I have lasagna rolls.

767
00:40:00.620 --> 00:40:03.020
I have an amazing chicken and rice soup.

768
00:40:03.180 --> 00:40:06.480
I also have brisket with all the sides.

769
00:40:06.800 --> 00:40:09.300
I also have chocolate chip muffins.

770
00:40:09.520 --> 00:40:15.040
I have millionaire bars, no-bake chocolate and oatmeal drops, an almond cake.

771
00:40:15.340 --> 00:40:17.580
So we're going to have a feast at lunch.

772
00:40:17.760 --> 00:40:23.120
You told me about this last night, and I've been distracted because I can't wait any longer.

773
00:40:23.120 --> 00:40:24.760
The coolers are sitting right next to me.

774
00:40:25.780 --> 00:40:26.720
We'll be right back.

775
00:40:38.780 --> 00:40:39.160
All right.

776
00:40:39.260 --> 00:40:39.960
We are back.

777
00:40:40.280 --> 00:40:41.660
Our bellies are full.

778
00:40:42.220 --> 00:40:46.460
I just ate the most delicious brisket with potatoes and carrots.

779
00:40:46.460 --> 00:40:48.720
And then I had a pecan cookie for dessert.

780
00:40:49.160 --> 00:40:50.000
Yes, Josh.

781
00:40:50.120 --> 00:40:51.080
You fed us all today.

782
00:40:51.320 --> 00:40:52.360
Thank you so much.

783
00:40:52.540 --> 00:40:56.080
And all of these things that I just ate are going to be in the cookbook, Ange?

784
00:40:56.220 --> 00:40:56.680
Yes.

785
00:40:56.880 --> 00:40:58.480
Oh, I know.

786
00:40:58.480 --> 00:40:59.860
This is going to be a good cookbook.

787
00:40:59.920 --> 00:41:01.340
It's looking so beautiful, too.

788
00:41:01.440 --> 00:41:02.740
I'm really excited about it.

789
00:41:03.020 --> 00:41:04.960
Well, we're going to head over to accounting.

790
00:41:05.440 --> 00:41:10.800
And Oscar and Angela, they need to try to smooth things over with Kevin.

791
00:41:11.100 --> 00:41:14.200
So they're going to present him with a gift from Philip.

792
00:41:14.720 --> 00:41:15.100
Yes.

793
00:41:15.280 --> 00:41:18.940
Philip got Kevin a $25 iTunes gift card.

794
00:41:19.200 --> 00:41:21.120
There's only $7 left on it.

795
00:41:21.400 --> 00:41:21.740
Yeah.

796
00:41:21.740 --> 00:41:22.700
But it's from Philip.

797
00:41:23.040 --> 00:41:25.640
And Kevin is very moved by this.

798
00:41:25.780 --> 00:41:30.000
He has a talking head where he says he and Philip are going to be best friends.

799
00:41:30.260 --> 00:41:30.440
Yeah.

800
00:41:30.680 --> 00:41:32.380
He's kind of standoffish at firSt.

801
00:41:32.540 --> 00:41:35.400
But, you know, he likes you when he starts buying you things.

802
00:41:35.700 --> 00:41:40.520
I mean, this is the advice that they give you if you're going to have a second kid.

803
00:41:40.920 --> 00:41:46.020
They say, have the baby buy a gift for the first kid.

804
00:41:46.240 --> 00:41:47.200
I've never heard that.

805
00:41:47.200 --> 00:41:47.680
To butter them up.

806
00:41:47.740 --> 00:41:48.560
Oh, you didn't know that?

807
00:41:48.580 --> 00:41:49.180
I didn't.

808
00:41:49.180 --> 00:41:49.960
Oh, my gosh.

809
00:41:50.080 --> 00:41:54.020
I mean, I remember I was five years old when my little sister Emily was born.

810
00:41:54.300 --> 00:41:58.200
And she gave me in the hospital when I visited her.

811
00:41:58.320 --> 00:42:00.460
My parents were like, Emily, got you this.

812
00:42:00.600 --> 00:42:02.040
And it was a little dog.

813
00:42:02.500 --> 00:42:04.000
And I named it Prancy.

814
00:42:04.300 --> 00:42:06.240
And it was my favorite stuffed animal.

815
00:42:06.240 --> 00:42:08.960
And I still have it on my shelf in my office today.

816
00:42:09.220 --> 00:42:14.460
And I guess when you're that little, you're not like, oh, the baby can't walk or speak.

817
00:42:14.640 --> 00:42:14.920
No.

818
00:42:15.060 --> 00:42:16.060
But bought me a gift.

819
00:42:16.240 --> 00:42:16.420
Yeah.

820
00:42:16.420 --> 00:42:18.800
I mean, I immediately liked her.

821
00:42:19.200 --> 00:42:21.620
I was like, this kid is terrific.

822
00:42:21.880 --> 00:42:24.480
This kid is really good at giving gifts.

823
00:42:24.600 --> 00:42:24.880
Yeah.

824
00:42:25.440 --> 00:42:27.860
Well, that says a lot about Kevin, where he's at.

825
00:42:28.040 --> 00:42:28.840
I know, right?

826
00:42:28.980 --> 00:42:30.260
About a five-year-old.

827
00:42:30.340 --> 00:42:30.540
Yeah.

828
00:42:31.140 --> 00:42:34.420
Well, the whole group has decided what they want to do with Daryl.

829
00:42:34.780 --> 00:42:38.060
They have decided they all want to dance with him.

830
00:42:38.560 --> 00:42:41.180
And Daryl says, all right, you're going to need some good speakers.

831
00:42:41.460 --> 00:42:42.160
Let's go.

832
00:42:42.160 --> 00:42:46.380
Oh, I do have a bulls**t card to play here in Daryl's office.

833
00:42:46.700 --> 00:42:47.320
What is it?

834
00:42:47.760 --> 00:42:48.660
Daryl left.

835
00:42:48.940 --> 00:42:51.140
He got his things and he left.

836
00:42:51.320 --> 00:42:53.820
And he had no plans to come back to his office, right?

837
00:42:54.020 --> 00:42:54.260
Sure.

838
00:42:54.660 --> 00:42:56.620
Well, guess what was sitting in the corner?

839
00:42:57.260 --> 00:42:57.740
What?

840
00:42:58.240 --> 00:42:59.940
His fakie bass fish thing.

841
00:43:00.280 --> 00:43:03.040
Oh, that he brought up from the warehouse to this?

842
00:43:03.040 --> 00:43:03.340
Yes.

843
00:43:03.480 --> 00:43:06.380
He left it behind, along with a box of some other things.

844
00:43:06.380 --> 00:43:09.060
But sitting right there is the fakie bass thing.

845
00:43:09.660 --> 00:43:10.560
You know what?

846
00:43:10.760 --> 00:43:13.960
I'm going to say he did that on purpose.

847
00:43:14.360 --> 00:43:16.120
He's not taking it with him.

848
00:43:16.260 --> 00:43:19.860
Maybe he doesn't need that in his snazzy new athlete office.

849
00:43:20.160 --> 00:43:20.800
I guess not.

850
00:43:20.940 --> 00:43:25.400
Maybe he's going for a new decor now that he's like a big deal agent guy.

851
00:43:25.540 --> 00:43:26.500
So he just left it.

852
00:43:26.580 --> 00:43:27.500
He's like, anyone want it?

853
00:43:27.580 --> 00:43:28.080
It's here.

854
00:43:28.920 --> 00:43:29.580
Yeah, maybe.

855
00:43:30.200 --> 00:43:32.340
Dwight has called Angela into his office.

856
00:43:32.760 --> 00:43:34.960
He tells her to close the door and have a seat.

857
00:43:36.380 --> 00:43:38.140
Philip may be a shrewd.

858
00:43:38.280 --> 00:43:44.580
And as such, Philip is entitled to a farm and inheritance and the right kind of upbringing,

859
00:43:44.580 --> 00:43:46.640
you know, under rigorous shrewd traditions.

860
00:43:47.120 --> 00:43:51.440
He also says that Angela will be compensated with a marriage proposal as well.

861
00:43:51.560 --> 00:43:53.880
It's kind of an afterthought to his speech.

862
00:43:54.640 --> 00:43:57.280
And there was a lot to play in this scene.

863
00:43:57.580 --> 00:43:58.100
Yeah.

864
00:43:58.440 --> 00:44:05.580
At first, Dave wanted me to be excited about the idea of Dwight raising Philip.

865
00:44:06.140 --> 00:44:11.280
But then when he says, and of course, a marriage proposal will be also offered to you, I'm

866
00:44:11.280 --> 00:44:14.880
supposed to then turn and be like, no, that's not how I want this.

867
00:44:15.420 --> 00:44:18.700
And so once again, Angela says, Philip is not your son.

868
00:44:18.860 --> 00:44:19.800
And she leaves.

869
00:44:20.220 --> 00:44:20.320
Yeah.

870
00:44:20.320 --> 00:44:29.400
Meanwhile, in the break room, Jim has brought Pam in, and there's a laptop sitting on one

871
00:44:29.400 --> 00:44:30.760
of the tables and a DVD.

872
00:44:31.580 --> 00:44:35.920
And it's clear, you know, he's going to play this DVD for her, but Dwight interrupts him.

873
00:44:36.200 --> 00:44:40.480
He throws the now beanbag at Jim's head and hits him right in the head.

874
00:44:40.680 --> 00:44:40.980
Yeah.

875
00:44:41.620 --> 00:44:42.140
Serious.

876
00:44:42.400 --> 00:44:42.840
Serious.

877
00:44:42.840 --> 00:44:46.220
So is Dwight just walking around with beanbags in his pockets?

878
00:44:47.520 --> 00:44:48.000
Yes.

879
00:44:48.220 --> 00:44:48.480
Yes.

880
00:44:48.700 --> 00:44:49.620
I believe he is.

881
00:44:50.260 --> 00:44:52.060
So Dwight says, I'll be right back.

882
00:44:52.140 --> 00:44:53.960
And Pam waits for a second.

883
00:44:54.340 --> 00:44:57.580
And then she is like, no, I got to see what this is.

884
00:44:57.640 --> 00:44:59.300
I'm going to put this DVD in the computer.

885
00:44:59.640 --> 00:45:00.620
She doesn't wait that long.

886
00:45:00.620 --> 00:45:01.300
She doesn't.

887
00:45:02.060 --> 00:45:06.760
And now we're going to cut back and forth between this scene with Jim and Dwight and

888
00:45:06.760 --> 00:45:09.160
what Pam is doing in the break room.

889
00:45:09.160 --> 00:45:12.320
I loved this scene, how it cut back and forth.

890
00:45:12.340 --> 00:45:13.420
I thought it was so beautiful.

891
00:45:13.640 --> 00:45:17.380
I loved seeing the footage of early Pam and Jim.

892
00:45:17.600 --> 00:45:19.760
It was really emotional to watch.

893
00:45:19.980 --> 00:45:26.360
It was emotional to watch as someone who worked on the show and was in these scenes to see

894
00:45:26.360 --> 00:45:30.200
the progression of the characters, but then as a fan and then as someone who's been re-watching

895
00:45:30.200 --> 00:45:31.200
them with her best friend.

896
00:45:31.200 --> 00:45:33.500
It was a very layered emotion for me.

897
00:45:35.020 --> 00:45:39.080
Well, we got a few fan questions about this montage that Pam is watching.

898
00:45:39.160 --> 00:45:40.860
One from Randy C.

899
00:45:41.000 --> 00:45:43.260
in Columbia, Maryland, who said,

900
00:45:43.480 --> 00:45:47.740
Jenna, when filming the sequence where Pam watches the DVD, was there anything really

901
00:45:47.740 --> 00:45:50.980
on the screen or was it put in during post-production?

902
00:45:51.400 --> 00:45:54.140
Oh, we also had a fan question from Angela K.

903
00:45:54.260 --> 00:45:54.980
in Los Angeles.

904
00:45:55.460 --> 00:45:56.300
What did she say?

905
00:45:56.820 --> 00:45:59.280
Jenna, were you actually watching the footage in the video?

906
00:45:59.520 --> 00:46:03.680
And may I also add that your performance in the scene is beautiful and made me tear up.

907
00:46:04.200 --> 00:46:05.620
I love it.

908
00:46:05.620 --> 00:46:11.820
Well, I'll have you both know, Randy and Angela K., I was watching it.

909
00:46:12.020 --> 00:46:15.040
And I was watching it for the first time.

910
00:46:15.180 --> 00:46:16.160
Oh, wow.

911
00:46:16.380 --> 00:46:17.560
When they started rolling.

912
00:46:17.960 --> 00:46:18.340
Oh.

913
00:46:18.620 --> 00:46:19.680
I knew what was coming.

914
00:46:19.680 --> 00:46:23.440
I knew I was going to see this montage of us as Jim and Pam, but-

915
00:46:23.440 --> 00:46:25.480
But there's no way to prepare yourself for it.

916
00:46:25.560 --> 00:46:25.960
There's not.

917
00:46:26.120 --> 00:46:27.560
Because there were so many wonderful moments.

918
00:46:27.820 --> 00:46:28.140
Mm-hmm.

919
00:46:28.980 --> 00:46:29.780
Sarah R.

920
00:46:30.040 --> 00:46:32.960
from Cleveland, Ohio, wanted to know who created the video.

921
00:46:32.960 --> 00:46:34.900
It was Claire Scanlon.

922
00:46:35.140 --> 00:46:35.460
Hmm.

923
00:46:35.720 --> 00:46:38.220
She collected the images.

924
00:46:38.720 --> 00:46:41.100
Dave Rogers gave her a few suggestions.

925
00:46:41.100 --> 00:46:43.360
There were a few suggestions in the script.

926
00:46:43.840 --> 00:46:47.040
She picked out the song, Open Your Eyes, from Snow Patrol.

927
00:46:47.860 --> 00:46:55.100
Dave said that he believes John Krasinski came in and worked on it a little bit with Claire.

928
00:46:55.340 --> 00:46:57.360
He knew what was on it, but I did not.

929
00:46:58.000 --> 00:47:01.460
But this was the last scene that we shot for this episode.

930
00:47:01.680 --> 00:47:02.600
So this was a Friday.

931
00:47:02.640 --> 00:47:03.680
It was evening.

932
00:47:03.780 --> 00:47:05.680
It was just us on the set.

933
00:47:05.820 --> 00:47:07.120
It was very quiet.

934
00:47:07.620 --> 00:47:10.280
Really kind of reminded me of when we did Casino Night,

935
00:47:10.280 --> 00:47:16.740
when everybody else had gone home and we had like a smaller group for this really emotional moment between these two characters.

936
00:47:17.140 --> 00:47:27.220
Well, I'd also love to give a big shout out to Dave Rogers for how he directed this, because I thought it was really beautiful that you watched all of this just heartfelt.

937
00:47:29.480 --> 00:47:37.040
And then Jim is talking to Dwight about Pam at the same time, and Dwight is thinking about Angela.

938
00:47:37.500 --> 00:47:46.540
When Jim tells Dwight to forget about fear and doubt and do whatever he can to get the one woman who's going to make everything worth it to just do it.

939
00:47:46.640 --> 00:47:48.260
And he says, you love Angela.

940
00:47:48.380 --> 00:47:49.920
You probably always have.

941
00:47:50.140 --> 00:47:51.700
And Rain is Dwight.

942
00:47:51.800 --> 00:47:53.340
His performance is brilliant, too.

943
00:47:53.440 --> 00:47:53.620
So good.

944
00:47:53.620 --> 00:47:56.860
He's tearing up, and he tells Jim he's a good assistant.

945
00:47:57.800 --> 00:48:01.560
So there's so much emotion happening in these two moments.

946
00:48:01.560 --> 00:48:02.840
I thought it was really well done.

947
00:48:03.160 --> 00:48:15.800
Well, this whole thing ends with Jim walking back into the break room, seeing that Pam has watched the video, and handing her the original teapot note.

948
00:48:15.800 --> 00:48:20.960
The one he took back and put in his pocket all of those years ago.

949
00:48:21.880 --> 00:48:23.980
We got a fan question from Lainey B.

950
00:48:24.220 --> 00:48:30.600
in Westerville, Ohio, who said, was it always the plan to revisit the teapot card Jim wrote for Pam?

951
00:48:30.860 --> 00:48:36.240
Or was it something that the writers remembered and wanted to incorporate into the episode?

952
00:48:37.220 --> 00:48:39.720
Lainey, it was not always planned.

953
00:48:40.000 --> 00:48:47.580
Like, not from when we did that first Christmas episode did anybody say, one day we're going to reveal what was on this note.

954
00:48:47.760 --> 00:48:49.840
It sort of took on a life of its own.

955
00:48:50.040 --> 00:48:55.840
People became so curious what was in the note that Jim didn't give Pam.

956
00:48:55.840 --> 00:49:06.220
And I've told this story before, but when we were pitching to Greg about season nine, I said, Greg, we have to read the teapot note.

957
00:49:06.460 --> 00:49:09.880
I mean, it's the greatest callback of the whole series.

958
00:49:10.140 --> 00:49:11.300
It's brilliant.

959
00:49:11.700 --> 00:49:13.700
This is a fantastic idea.

960
00:49:14.220 --> 00:49:16.260
Did they lose their minds when you pitched it?

961
00:49:16.480 --> 00:49:20.780
Well, I felt like I kind of had to convince them a little bit because—

962
00:49:20.780 --> 00:49:20.980
What?

963
00:49:21.460 --> 00:49:22.460
Here's the thing.

964
00:49:22.460 --> 00:49:32.560
Greg said, I don't think we'll ever be able to write anything that would satisfy everybody's imagination of this note after all these years.

965
00:49:32.760 --> 00:49:40.480
Like, the beauty of the note is that as the audience member, you've written the perfect thing on the note.

966
00:49:40.660 --> 00:49:41.440
That's brilliant.

967
00:49:41.640 --> 00:49:43.220
I mean, he's so smart, right?

968
00:49:43.400 --> 00:49:44.860
That is a very smart take.

969
00:49:45.220 --> 00:49:47.500
But it's enough for me to just see it.

970
00:49:47.740 --> 00:49:49.300
Well, that was the thing.

971
00:49:49.540 --> 00:49:52.080
We didn't have to read it to you.

972
00:49:52.460 --> 00:49:56.000
You just needed the satisfaction of Pam getting it.

973
00:49:56.080 --> 00:49:57.180
She finally gets it.

974
00:49:57.180 --> 00:49:58.020
Of Pam reading it.

975
00:49:58.080 --> 00:49:58.320
Yes.

976
00:49:58.540 --> 00:50:02.760
And then you still get to fill in the blank, but you get that closure.

977
00:50:03.120 --> 00:50:05.260
I've told this story before, too.

978
00:50:05.560 --> 00:50:08.240
But John Krasinski wrote the note.

979
00:50:08.340 --> 00:50:10.260
It was a private note.

980
00:50:10.500 --> 00:50:13.200
That he wrote to you as Jenna.

981
00:50:13.600 --> 00:50:13.980
Correct.

982
00:50:14.200 --> 00:50:16.620
About being your scene partner all those years.

983
00:50:16.620 --> 00:50:17.040
Yes.

984
00:50:17.300 --> 00:50:21.600
So it was kind of like a goodbye, thank you note.

985
00:50:21.900 --> 00:50:23.400
I still have it.

986
00:50:23.560 --> 00:50:24.480
It's private.

987
00:50:24.960 --> 00:50:26.880
I'll never reveal what was in it.

988
00:50:26.980 --> 00:50:31.040
But it was a beautiful, beautiful note from one scene partner to another.

989
00:50:31.620 --> 00:50:32.860
It made me cry.

990
00:50:32.860 --> 00:50:36.680
So when you see me crying in the episode, those are my real tears.

991
00:50:36.680 --> 00:50:45.780
I did notice when you were reading it, it seemed like some genuine surprise that you didn't know what was going to be given to you.

992
00:50:46.260 --> 00:50:46.780
Yes.

993
00:50:46.920 --> 00:50:53.560
They told me they were going to let John write the note, but I thought he was going to write it as Jim to Pam.

994
00:50:54.340 --> 00:50:58.900
So as I'm reading it and I'm realizing that it says, Dear Jenna,

995
00:50:59.420 --> 00:51:06.840
and then it's just this beautiful letter about what it has meant for us to work together and be these scene partners.

996
00:51:07.080 --> 00:51:09.900
And, you know, it's like to be Jim and Pam.

997
00:51:10.300 --> 00:51:10.580
Yeah.

998
00:51:10.860 --> 00:51:10.980
Yeah.

999
00:51:11.120 --> 00:51:12.360
I was like, oh, my gosh.

1000
00:51:12.520 --> 00:51:13.360
Oh, oh, my goodness.

1001
00:51:13.600 --> 00:51:13.780
Yeah.

1002
00:51:13.880 --> 00:51:16.940
You see your face goes through a bunch of realizations.

1003
00:51:17.320 --> 00:51:17.500
Yeah.

1004
00:51:17.500 --> 00:51:23.120
And I think you can see John watching me read it.

1005
00:51:23.340 --> 00:51:23.460
Yes.

1006
00:51:23.460 --> 00:51:25.200
I think you see him.

1007
00:51:25.360 --> 00:51:25.900
For sure.

1008
00:51:26.160 --> 00:51:29.400
Like, okay, I'm standing here as she reads this letter.

1009
00:51:29.740 --> 00:51:30.060
Yeah.

1010
00:51:30.440 --> 00:51:30.660
Yeah.

1011
00:51:31.100 --> 00:51:32.540
Well, I agree with Greg.

1012
00:51:32.920 --> 00:51:41.340
I'm glad you've never shared what you actually read, what was from John to you, because I don't want to break that fourth wall.

1013
00:51:41.340 --> 00:51:47.880
I want to imagine this moment between Jim and Pam, and I want to imagine what I would have wanted Jim to say.

1014
00:51:49.140 --> 00:51:53.680
Well, as a wrap gift at the finale, I wanted to return the favor.

1015
00:51:53.720 --> 00:52:07.440
So I bought a little teapot, and I wrote a note to John from Jenna, my own little teapot note to him, thanking him for all those years working together and what it had meant to me.

1016
00:52:07.440 --> 00:52:12.700
I mean, I'll never have a scene partner like that again.

1017
00:52:12.900 --> 00:52:18.340
I feel like I have a work partner and a creative partner like that here with you, Angela.

1018
00:52:18.480 --> 00:52:18.660
Yeah.

1019
00:52:18.660 --> 00:52:25.100
This is like another moment of, like, chemistry that you just – you can't plan for it.

1020
00:52:25.160 --> 00:52:26.640
It's just, like, it's perfect.

1021
00:52:26.640 --> 00:52:36.160
But to go on a nine-year journey with another actor in such an intimate and vulnerable way, as our characters do, it's pretty special.

1022
00:52:36.340 --> 00:52:37.820
I mean, we're bonded for life, for sure.

1023
00:52:37.980 --> 00:52:38.480
For sure.

1024
00:52:38.840 --> 00:52:40.120
It's nine years.

1025
00:52:40.360 --> 00:52:40.660
Mm-hmm.

1026
00:52:41.120 --> 00:52:42.500
Yeah, I feel the same way.

1027
00:52:42.840 --> 00:52:49.880
And, you know, we reached out to Dave about this episode, and he shared something about this scene, about how they would cut from Jim and Dwight back to Pam.

1028
00:52:49.880 --> 00:52:55.100
And it's something he fought for, and that was to keep the music playing under the cutaways.

1029
00:52:55.420 --> 00:52:56.080
Mm-hmm.

1030
00:52:56.400 --> 00:53:00.200
And at first, I guess Greg wanted to take it out whenever they cut to Jim and Dwight.

1031
00:53:00.420 --> 00:53:08.380
But Claire agreed with Dave, and they said it would be a great tool that the documentary filmmakers could use to underscore these two moments.

1032
00:53:08.380 --> 00:53:11.200
And he really felt like it tied the two scenes together.

1033
00:53:11.480 --> 00:53:14.960
And ultimately, Greg agreed, and they kept the music throughout both scenes.

1034
00:53:15.460 --> 00:53:17.960
And I do think it tied them together beautifully.

1035
00:53:18.660 --> 00:53:23.940
So there's one more thing about this scene that caused quite a stir after it aired.

1036
00:53:25.040 --> 00:53:28.920
And it all has to do with a little mistake that they made.

1037
00:53:29.460 --> 00:53:35.600
You know that very first slide of the little slideshow where Jim has written a note to Pam?

1038
00:53:35.840 --> 00:53:37.060
Yes, he wrote Beasley,

1039
00:53:37.540 --> 00:53:38.820
You think I'll have regrets.

1040
00:53:38.820 --> 00:53:43.720
I asked the doc crew to help me show you why I won't dot, dot, dot.

1041
00:53:44.080 --> 00:53:44.560
Mm-hmm.

1042
00:53:45.360 --> 00:53:49.780
Well, when this episode first aired, Pam's last name was misspelled.

1043
00:53:50.160 --> 00:53:54.180
It was spelled B-E-E-S-L-E-Y.

1044
00:53:54.440 --> 00:53:56.500
But that's not how you spell Beasley.

1045
00:53:57.300 --> 00:54:01.760
You spell it B-E-E-S-L-Y.

1046
00:54:02.040 --> 00:54:02.460
Mm.

1047
00:54:02.860 --> 00:54:03.700
No E.

1048
00:54:03.980 --> 00:54:04.620
No E.

1049
00:54:04.620 --> 00:54:12.160
And fans were like, Jim misspelled his wife's name in the love tribute video.

1050
00:54:13.980 --> 00:54:19.720
But then people started arguing over what is the correct spelling of Pam's last name.

1051
00:54:20.060 --> 00:54:22.340
There's been several versions online.

1052
00:54:22.340 --> 00:54:25.460
Well, here's what I can tell you.

1053
00:54:25.820 --> 00:54:30.780
In the very first page of every script, there's a cast liSt.

1054
00:54:30.940 --> 00:54:39.800
And from script number one to script finale, Pam's last name has always been spelled B-E-E-S-L-Y.

1055
00:54:40.020 --> 00:54:41.540
That's how Greg intended it.

1056
00:54:41.780 --> 00:54:45.620
However, it is misspelled a couple of times in early episodes.

1057
00:54:45.620 --> 00:54:55.880
And then in this episode, but in business school at her art fair, it's spelled correctly in the credits for Threat Level Midnight.

1058
00:54:56.080 --> 00:55:03.460
It's spelled correctly on the big poster outside their wedding where they misspelled Jim's last name, Halprit.

1059
00:55:04.080 --> 00:55:05.760
Her name is spelled correctly.

1060
00:55:06.140 --> 00:55:08.120
So that is the correct spelling.

1061
00:55:08.580 --> 00:55:09.420
You heard it here.

1062
00:55:09.560 --> 00:55:10.560
You heard it here.

1063
00:55:10.680 --> 00:55:15.200
And Angela, when you made us those matching blue hoodies, you spelled it correctly.

1064
00:55:15.200 --> 00:55:15.840
I did.

1065
00:55:16.160 --> 00:55:18.660
So I have a hoodie with Beasley on the back.

1066
00:55:19.460 --> 00:55:23.460
I don't know how this happened exactly, but it has been corrected.

1067
00:55:23.540 --> 00:55:25.680
So they corrected it for the DVDs.

1068
00:55:25.780 --> 00:55:27.120
They corrected it for syndication.

1069
00:55:27.120 --> 00:55:30.060
But on the first viewing, it was spelled wrong.

1070
00:55:30.720 --> 00:55:35.960
But, you know, there were some people who tried to justify it by saying, hey, Jim didn't make the video.

1071
00:55:36.180 --> 00:55:37.580
The doc crew made the video.

1072
00:55:37.740 --> 00:55:37.920
Right.

1073
00:55:38.020 --> 00:55:40.560
The doc crew didn't know how to spell her last name.

1074
00:55:40.800 --> 00:55:44.920
But also doesn't inspire a lot of confidence in the doc crew.

1075
00:55:45.200 --> 00:55:46.420
To get everything right.

1076
00:55:46.680 --> 00:55:49.080
I mean, literally what they're supposed to do is just document.

1077
00:55:49.700 --> 00:55:53.560
Well, let's move to the bullpen because Aaron is cuing up the music.

1078
00:55:53.740 --> 00:55:54.780
There are disco lights.

1079
00:55:54.940 --> 00:55:56.200
It is time to dance.

1080
00:55:56.580 --> 00:55:59.480
And I thought you might like a breakdown of this dance sequence.

1081
00:55:59.700 --> 00:56:00.080
Please.

1082
00:56:00.680 --> 00:56:07.320
When the music starts, the kitchen door opens and Daryl is standing with his back to the bullpen and a leather jacket with his collar up.

1083
00:56:07.440 --> 00:56:08.360
He spins around.

1084
00:56:08.680 --> 00:56:10.760
His first dance move is with Stanley.

1085
00:56:11.040 --> 00:56:13.920
They do a series of handshakes and then chest bump.

1086
00:56:14.320 --> 00:56:17.080
Then Daryl and Nelly have a very sexy dance.

1087
00:56:17.080 --> 00:56:21.540
I was like, that's a couple I hadn't thought of before.

1088
00:56:21.540 --> 00:56:22.080
I know.

1089
00:56:22.860 --> 00:56:23.420
Okay.

1090
00:56:24.140 --> 00:56:27.500
Then Daryl and Toby do a dance where, what are they doing?

1091
00:56:27.640 --> 00:56:28.920
Are they pretending to ride a pony?

1092
00:56:29.180 --> 00:56:30.920
Are they pretending to rope a cow?

1093
00:56:31.220 --> 00:56:31.900
It's like the.

1094
00:56:32.240 --> 00:56:32.700
Yeah.

1095
00:56:32.800 --> 00:56:34.600
Are they lassoing one another?

1096
00:56:34.880 --> 00:56:35.140
One another.

1097
00:56:35.420 --> 00:56:36.040
I don't know.

1098
00:56:36.640 --> 00:56:42.940
Then Daryl and Phyllis do a really fancy choreographed kickball change spin, then a dip move.

1099
00:56:43.500 --> 00:56:45.780
Phyllis was having fun in real life in that moment.

1100
00:56:45.940 --> 00:56:46.240
For sure.

1101
00:56:46.420 --> 00:56:48.100
All of her dancing days coming back.

1102
00:56:48.580 --> 00:56:51.080
Then Daryl does air guitar with Creed.

1103
00:56:51.520 --> 00:56:58.760
And then with Meredith, Daryl doesn't really dance, but Meredith waves her hand over his crotch and then spanks his butt.

1104
00:56:59.780 --> 00:57:03.720
And then at 17 minutes and 23 seconds is my favorite.

1105
00:57:03.980 --> 00:57:05.480
It is Daryl, Kevin, and Oscar.

1106
00:57:05.880 --> 00:57:09.720
They're doing a very Madonna, Vogue kind of dance moment.

1107
00:57:09.880 --> 00:57:11.060
It is epic.

1108
00:57:11.680 --> 00:57:18.380
Then Pam and Jim and Daryl and Aaron form a line and dance, kind of like a, I don't know what that was, a Congo line kind of thing?

1109
00:57:18.520 --> 00:57:18.880
Kind of.

1110
00:57:19.140 --> 00:57:24.420
And then it ends with Daryl and Aaron spinning in a circle, holding hands in front of reception.

1111
00:57:25.160 --> 00:57:27.440
We got a lot of fan questions about this.

1112
00:57:27.860 --> 00:57:33.200
People wanted to know if music was really playing or if we had to do that thing where you're dancing to silence.

1113
00:57:33.440 --> 00:57:33.820
Oh, yeah.

1114
00:57:34.280 --> 00:57:35.700
Music was really playing.

1115
00:57:35.820 --> 00:57:40.440
It was playing low, but we had it so that we could dance to the beat.

1116
00:57:40.440 --> 00:57:44.140
But there were two rehearsals for this.

1117
00:57:44.560 --> 00:57:52.900
Our resident choreographer, Marianne Kellogg, came in, and she worked with each person on what their dance with Daryl might be.

1118
00:57:53.480 --> 00:57:55.140
So that was all planned.

1119
00:57:55.140 --> 00:57:58.620
It wasn't like we just did impromptu moves or anything.

1120
00:57:59.040 --> 00:58:02.000
Dave said it was actually a fairly complicated scene.

1121
00:58:02.000 --> 00:58:07.680
It had to be carefully choreographed with camera and with Daryl's movement through the office.

1122
00:58:07.800 --> 00:58:12.520
He wanted to make sure that everyone was in focus and had their moment.

1123
00:58:12.520 --> 00:58:23.080
The moment we had a fan catch that I know about from personal experience, it was Kelly Y from Carver, Massachusetts, who said,

1124
00:58:23.080 --> 00:58:29.280
I have been waiting nine seasons to tell you this background catch that I notice every time I watch this episode.

1125
00:58:29.740 --> 00:58:40.040
At 17 minutes and 45 seconds, when Jim and Pam are dancing in the background, you can very clearly see John mess up his dance move and mouth the words, oh, s**t.

1126
00:58:42.780 --> 00:58:45.560
Kelly said this makes me crack up every time.

1127
00:58:47.400 --> 00:58:51.800
Kelly, John and I were added to this dance scene at the very last minute.

1128
00:58:53.080 --> 00:59:07.000
I don't know why, but we had not been at the other rehearsals, and so on the day, we got our dance moves, and we were so clumsy, and we kept messing up,

1129
00:59:07.040 --> 00:59:12.740
and we were cracking ourselves up, because we were like, why can't we do this simple dance move?

1130
00:59:15.180 --> 00:59:22.980
But this is just such a wonderfully joyous moment in this finale before the finale.

1131
00:59:23.080 --> 00:59:28.360
And Daryl has a talking head where he says, you know, leaving quietly seemed dignified,

1132
00:59:28.360 --> 00:59:35.880
but having Kevin grind up on his front while Aaron pretended to hump him from behind is a more accurate tribute to his years there.

1133
00:59:36.080 --> 00:59:38.320
He said he's really going to miss these guys.

1134
00:59:38.560 --> 00:59:38.880
Yeah.

1135
00:59:41.310 --> 00:59:41.630
Ah.

1136
00:59:42.930 --> 00:59:49.370
Well, now everyone's leaving for the day, and they say, we'll see you at Poor Richard's, because remember, everybody's going to gather at Poor Richard's,

1137
00:59:49.410 --> 00:59:51.830
because the documentary is airing tonight.

1138
00:59:52.970 --> 00:59:55.530
Oscar says, I'll drop Philip off at my mother's.

1139
00:59:55.650 --> 00:59:59.310
I love that Oscar's mother is Philip's babysitter.

1140
00:59:59.490 --> 00:59:59.870
I know.

1141
01:00:00.010 --> 01:00:00.550
How cute.

1142
01:00:00.550 --> 01:00:05.790
And he says, Angela, you can't come with me, because I don't want my mother to know I'm living with a straight woman and get her hopes up.

1143
01:00:06.010 --> 01:00:06.210
Yeah.

1144
01:00:07.430 --> 01:00:10.910
So Angela is going to drive to Poor Richard's by herself.

1145
01:00:11.990 --> 01:00:15.790
But, oh boy, Dwight comes out of nowhere.

1146
01:00:16.170 --> 01:00:21.630
He's got a siren on top of his car and a bullhorn, and he's shouting at her to pull over.

1147
01:00:22.730 --> 01:00:24.870
Well, I have a lot to say about this scene.

1148
01:00:25.410 --> 01:00:28.510
We wanted it to feel as real as possible.

1149
01:00:29.110 --> 01:00:29.590
Okay.

1150
01:00:29.730 --> 01:00:32.670
Well, we have a fan question from Rachel S.

1151
01:00:32.770 --> 01:00:37.650
in Shetland, Scotland, who wanted to know, how did you do this driving scene and pulling over?

1152
01:00:37.810 --> 01:00:41.830
Because I think that's you in the car, Angela.

1153
01:00:42.070 --> 01:00:42.610
Am I right?

1154
01:00:42.610 --> 01:00:43.950
It looks like you.

1155
01:00:44.190 --> 01:00:45.130
You are correct.

1156
01:00:45.130 --> 01:00:50.410
So we did a combination, and I have a lot of pictures from this particular scene.

1157
01:00:50.410 --> 01:00:52.390
I brought my camera to set that day.

1158
01:00:52.970 --> 01:00:55.430
And also Steve Burgess took a bunch of pictures.

1159
01:00:55.910 --> 01:01:01.110
We did a combination of my car being pulled where I'm not driving.

1160
01:01:01.270 --> 01:01:02.510
That's in the straightaways.

1161
01:01:03.150 --> 01:01:06.510
And then where we pull off the road is really me.

1162
01:01:06.930 --> 01:01:07.890
That's what I thought.

1163
01:01:08.050 --> 01:01:09.090
Yes, that's really me.

1164
01:01:09.270 --> 01:01:11.790
When he cuts you off and kind of forces your car off the road.

1165
01:01:11.930 --> 01:01:12.110
Right.

1166
01:01:12.230 --> 01:01:15.590
Because it had to be us because I get out of the car so quickly.

1167
01:01:15.710 --> 01:01:17.210
And they had cameras everywhere.

1168
01:01:17.390 --> 01:01:18.850
They had cameras mounted to the car.

1169
01:01:18.850 --> 01:01:22.930
They had lipstick cameras inside so they could catch a bunch of different angles.

1170
01:01:23.130 --> 01:01:27.010
And then we also did have stunt drivers for when the cars were going really fast on the straightaway.

1171
01:01:27.390 --> 01:01:27.630
Okay.

1172
01:01:27.950 --> 01:01:28.170
Okay.

1173
01:01:28.510 --> 01:01:32.530
Well, when you get out of that car, Angela, talk about coming in hot.

1174
01:01:32.910 --> 01:01:33.390
Well...

1175
01:01:33.390 --> 01:01:34.650
You are pissed.

1176
01:01:34.870 --> 01:01:35.670
I'm pissed.

1177
01:01:35.850 --> 01:01:37.450
I just got run off the road.

1178
01:01:37.750 --> 01:01:39.410
I mean, you'd be pissed off.

1179
01:01:39.670 --> 01:01:39.930
Yeah.

1180
01:01:40.250 --> 01:01:41.390
So here's the thing.

1181
01:01:41.950 --> 01:01:45.870
When we did the first take of this, it just felt a little stiff.

1182
01:01:46.370 --> 01:01:46.510
Right?

1183
01:01:47.570 --> 01:01:54.390
And Dave and Brent were like, you guys, why don't you, at the beginning, improvise to get into the main beat of the scene.

1184
01:01:54.510 --> 01:01:54.790
Okay.

1185
01:01:54.790 --> 01:01:55.390
To the proposal.

1186
01:01:55.690 --> 01:01:56.750
So we were like, okay.

1187
01:01:57.570 --> 01:02:03.310
So I thought I would play you the scene and then I'll tell you the parts that were improvised.

1188
01:02:03.590 --> 01:02:03.830
Okay.

1189
01:02:04.090 --> 01:02:04.590
Great.

1190
01:02:04.590 --> 01:02:27.670
Oh, it's not my son.

1191
01:02:27.910 --> 01:02:33.150
I will raise a hundred children with a hundred of your lovers if it means I can be with you.

1192
01:02:33.510 --> 01:02:36.440
Can you put that down?

1193
01:02:36.660 --> 01:02:39.580
This expresses how loudly I love you.

1194
01:02:47.110 --> 01:02:54.550
This is a ring taken from the buttocks of my grandmother, put there by the gangster patriarch of the Coors dynasty,

1195
01:02:55.750 --> 01:02:58.410
melted in a foundry run by Mennonites.

1196
01:02:58.610 --> 01:02:58.870
Okay.

1197
01:02:59.230 --> 01:02:59.530
Yes.

1198
01:02:59.790 --> 01:03:00.770
Yes, I will.

1199
01:03:05.180 --> 01:03:06.320
I love you.

1200
01:03:06.620 --> 01:03:07.400
I love you.

1201
01:03:09.570 --> 01:03:10.810
And I lied to you.

1202
01:03:11.010 --> 01:03:11.210
What?

1203
01:03:12.730 --> 01:03:13.810
Phillip's your son.

1204
01:03:14.150 --> 01:03:14.570
What?

1205
01:03:14.710 --> 01:03:15.550
Why would you say that?

1206
01:03:15.550 --> 01:03:19.910
I just needed you to want to marry me because you wanted to marry me.

1207
01:03:22.230 --> 01:03:23.270
Get out!

1208
01:03:24.150 --> 01:03:25.110
I'm a dad!

1209
01:03:25.290 --> 01:03:25.930
You're a dad!

1210
01:03:29.190 --> 01:03:30.710
Oh, it's so good.

1211
01:03:31.670 --> 01:03:34.030
It's so fun to rewatch that.

1212
01:03:34.030 --> 01:03:35.150
I have to tell you guys.

1213
01:03:35.150 --> 01:03:39.350
We really, man, we put our hearts out there for that scene.

1214
01:03:39.490 --> 01:03:43.310
So the parts that are improvised are, Dwight, what the f*** is your problem?

1215
01:03:43.690 --> 01:03:44.130
Yes.

1216
01:03:44.590 --> 01:03:46.510
And then him saying, shut up, woman.

1217
01:03:46.890 --> 01:03:47.290
Okay.

1218
01:03:47.730 --> 01:03:49.690
And then me saying, who drives like that?

1219
01:03:49.770 --> 01:03:51.230
And him saying, listen to me.

1220
01:03:53.030 --> 01:03:57.790
Then we start the scripted part with, I love you and I don't care that Phillip's not my son.

1221
01:03:58.630 --> 01:04:01.630
